29. The Committee is concerned about reports that the police systematically abuse children. The Committee recalls its general comment No. 13 (2011) (a Committee guidance document) and recommends that the State Party strengthen its efforts:

(a) To continue to implement the Juvenile Justice System Act, 2018 (a law on the justice system for children), which exempts children under the age of 18 years from punishment for hudud offences (offences under Islamic criminal law with fixed punishments);

(b) To start an independent inquiry into all allegations of torture and ill-treatment of children, especially those committed by law enforcement and security officers;

(c) To enforce the prohibition of torture and ensure that allegations of torture or other cruel, inhuman or degrading treatment or punishment of children are properly investigated. The State Party should also ensure that perpetrators are punished in a way that matches the seriousness of their acts and that children who are victims receive adequate remedies (proper ways to repair the harm);

(d) To ensure that children have access to confidential, child-friendly complaint mechanisms (safe ways to report abuse) for reporting torture and ill-treatment in detention facilities;

(e) To enforce the Zainab Alert, Response and Recovery Act, 2020 (a law on alerts, response and recovery for missing children). The State Party should also speed up the adoption of the bill aimed at criminalizing enforced disappearance (making it a crime to secretly detain a person and hide what happened to them), ensure that persons under the age of 18 years are not subjected to arbitrary detention (detention without a proper legal reason), and promptly and effectively investigate all cases of enforced disappearance of children.

